                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                        "entry_id": "05ffedd0-cc99-3341-9fff-13f0c8d58647",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The argument \\\"cogito, ergo sum\\\" (\\\"I think therefore I am\\\") from the philosophy of René Descartes; the mental act of thinking this thought; a conscious being which performs this mental act."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "capitalized",
                            "sometimes",
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b4217e9-8cd7-4e17-a367-1f4fd989b345",
                                "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                                "text": "The Cogito of Descartes and Husserl is an apprehension of fact. […] Such a Cogito is performed by a consciousness directed upon consciousness, a consciousness which takes consciousness as an object."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8faf498c-a759-4f83-96c2-08d6331fa27b",
                                "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                                "text": "But are there not as many consciousnesses or cogitos as there are individuals?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7fd4c45c-ce84-429b-a090-339230cbf3fe",
                                "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                                "text": "An obvious candidate for this class of propositions would be the cogito, whose evidence, Descartes insisted, is not founded on inference."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f279a50d-b729-4fde-8f39-d24c0c57891a",
                                "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                                "text": "Benhabib proposes a […] \"recognition that the subjects of reason are finite, embodied and fragile creatures, and not disembodied cogitos or abstract unities of transcendental apperception\"."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "681ae99d-ae98-470a-8e69-8452fdb3df6f",
                                "sense_id": "f19f5003-1c19-3fb0-bf81-b01e0dc1b5e0",
                                "text": "It may be thought that this leads to an even more radical skepticism than that envisaged by Descartes, since now even the cogito may be questioned."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
